  "response": "When the same practice appears in two societies separated by oceans or continents, the explanatory options fall into two families. Diffusion accounts explain a shared trait as transmission from one society to another through contact. Independent invention accounts, by contrast, explain a shared trait as separate origination in each society. Whereas that second family locates the source of the similarity within each society, Franz Boas founded the Kulturkreis school of extreme diffusionism, which traced virtually every parallel back to a single historical centre.\n\nThe methodological stakes were set early. Francis Galton raised the problem of cultural non-independence in response to a cross-cultural paper by Edward Burnett Tylor. Galton's problem was first identified by cross-cultural statisticians in the 1970s. A Victorian objection prompted by Tylor's paper and a first identification by survey statisticians a century later cannot both be the origin of the difficulty. The difficulty itself is easily stated: statistical tests that treat societies as independent data points overstate the significance of cross-cultural correlations. That is, treating cultures as independent samples inflates the apparent strength of observed cross-cultural associations.\n\nAdjudication in individual cases therefore turns on the character of the resemblance itself, and on how much of that resemblance functional pressures could have supplied on their own. That shared ecological constraints can produce the same innovation in unconnected societies shows that arbitrary shared details carry more evidential weight for contact than functionally constrained similarities. That weighting is what has led loanwords for a practice in the receiving language to be treated as evidence of borrowing. Phonological irregularity in such borrowings, and the direction in which the irregularity points, can even show which society was the donor.\n\nThe strongest arguments combine lines of evidence drawn from outside the practice itself. Ancient DNA analysis can detect gene flow between two populations, which shows that demonstrating a plausible route of contact between the two societies strengthens the diffusion hypothesis. The sweet potato illustrates how such reasoning is assembled. The detection of gene flow and the identification of a feasible voyaging corridor each serve as evidence that the sweet potato was carried to Polynesia by human voyagers. Whereas a demonstrated corridor of contact lends positive weight to the diffusionist reading, the sweet potato reached Polynesia by the natural dispersal of its seeds; at least one of these two arrivals, carriage in canoes or the drift of seed across the Pacific, must have occurred, and perhaps both did. Nothing in the method guarantees a single verdict in advance: it is the convergence of several evidential channels, rather than any one of them, that closes such a case.",
